Title: Your Own Types — Koka Tutorial
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]

# Your Own Types { .heading-no-number }

So far we've used built-in types like `int`, `string`, and `list`. Now let's define
our own types to model the data our programs work with.

---
```koka
import std/num/float64
```

## Structs: Named Fields

A `struct` groups related values together under a name:

```koka
struct point
  x : int
  y : int

fun example1()
  val p = Point(3, 4)
  println(p.x.show ++ ", " ++ p.y.show)   // 3, 4
```

Koka automatically generates:
- A constructor `Point(x, y)` (uppercase name of the struct)
- Field accessors: `p.x`, `p.y` (dot notation, just regular functions)

### Named and Default Constructor Arguments

You can use named arguments in the constructor:

```koka
struct person
  name : string
  age  : int
  email : string = ""   // default value

fun example2()
  val alice = Person(name = "Alice", age = 30)
  val bob   = Person("Bob", 25, email = "bob@example.com")
  println(alice.name)    // Alice
  println(bob.email)     // bob@example.com
```

### Updating Structs

All structs are **immutable**. To produce a changed copy, use the application syntax —
passing named fields to override:

```koka
fun have-birthday( p : person ) : person
  p( age = p.age + 1 )

fun example3()
  val alice = Person("Alice", 30)
  val older = alice.have-birthday
  println(older.age.show)   // 31
  println(alice.age.show)   // 30 — original unchanged!
```

The expression `p(age = p.age + 1)` creates a new `:person` equal to `p` with `age` updated.
There is no special record-update subsystem behind this: Koka keeps to its "functions all the way down"
style, where named/default arguments and ordinary function application explain the feature.

You can think of a `struct` as convenient syntax for a regular single-constructor algebraic data type.
For example, the earlier `person` declaration corresponds closely to a `type` with one constructor `Person`.

In desugared form, this is the same shape:

```unchecked
struct person
  age : int
  name : string
  realname : string = name

// roughly equivalent to:
type person
  Person
    age : int
    name : string
    realname : string = name
```

---

## Sum Types: Multiple Shapes

Sometimes a value can be one of several different things. A `type` with multiple
constructors represents this:

```koka
type shape
  Circle( radius   : float64 )
  Rectangle( width : float64, height : float64 )
  Triangle( base   : float64, height : float64 )
```

Values are created with the constructor name:

```koka
fun area( s : shape ) : float64
  match s
    Circle(r)        -> 3.14159 * r * r
    Rectangle(w, h)  -> w * h
    Triangle(b, h)   -> 0.5 * b * h

fun example4()
  val c = Circle(5.0)
  val r = Rectangle(4.0, 6.0)
  println(area(c).show)   // ~78.5
  println(area(r).show)   // 24.0
```

The `match` expression branches on which constructor was used. The compiler
checks that every constructor is handled — if you add `Triangle` later and
forget to update `area`, you get a compile-time warning.

Some built-in types are just small special cases of the same idea:

- `void` has no constructors, so there are no values of type `void`
- `()` has exactly one constructor `()`
- `bool` has exactly two constructors: `False` and `True`

---

## Pattern Matching in Depth

Patterns can:

**Bind variables** — name the fields you need:
```koka
fun describe( s : shape ) : string
  match s
    Circle(r)        -> "circle with radius " ++ r.show
    Rectangle(w, h)  -> "rectangle " ++ w.show ++ " x " ++ h.show
    _                -> "other shape"
```

**Use guards** — add boolean conditions after `|`:
```koka
fun classify-circle( s : shape ) : string
  match s
    Circle(r) | r > 10.0 -> "large circle"
    Circle(r) | r > 1.0  -> "medium circle"
    Circle(_)             -> "small circle"
    _                     -> "not a circle"
```

**Nest patterns** — destructure values within values:
```koka
fun first-two( xs : list<int> ) : string
  match xs
    Cons(a, Cons(b, _)) -> "first two: " ++ a.show ++ ", " ++ b.show
    Cons(a, Nil)        -> "only one: " ++ a.show
    Nil                 -> "empty"
```

---

## Recursive Types

Types can refer to themselves, enabling tree-like structures:

```koka
type tree<a>
  Leaf
  Node( left : tree<a>, value : a, right : tree<a> )

fun tree-sum( t : tree<int> ) : int
  match t
    Leaf           -> 0
    Node(l, v, r)  -> tree-sum(l) + v + tree-sum(r)

fun example5()
  val t = Node(Node(Leaf, 1, Leaf), 2, Node(Leaf, 3, Leaf))
  println(tree-sum(t).show)   // 6
```

This is the same algebraic style used by Koka's standard `list<a>` type: a list is either empty (`Nil`) or a
head element paired with another list (`Cons`). Accessors are generated for named constructor fields just as
they are for struct fields.

For reasoning about recursion, Koka distinguishes declaration forms:

- `type` for finite inductive data types
- `co type` for covariant recursive definitions (the type appears in )
- `div type` for unrestricted recursive definitions

Most everyday modeling uses plain `type`; the other forms are for advanced recursive encodings.

---

## `maybe<a>`: Optional Values

Koka's standard library has `maybe<a>` for values that might not exist:

```unchecked
type maybe<a>
  Nothing
  Just( value : a )
```

Instead of returning a special "null" or "sentinel" value, functions return
`Nothing` or `Just(x)`:

```unchecked
fun safe-divide( x : int, y : int ) : maybe<int>
  if y == 0 then Nothing
  else Just(x / y)

fun example6()
  match safe-divide(10, 2)
    Just(n) -> println(n.show)    // 5
    Nothing -> println("error")

  // Or use .default to provide a fallback:
  println(safe-divide(10, 0).default(42).show)   // 42
```

---

## Summary

In this section you learned:
- `struct` for grouping named fields together
- Constructor naming (uppercase), field accessors, default values
- Updating structs with the application syntax `p(field = value)`
- `type` with multiple constructors for "one of many" values
- Pattern matching: binding, guards, nesting
- Recursive types for tree structures
- `maybe<a>` for optional values

> For details on value types, boxing, and runtime representation, see the
> [Types & Representation Reference](../reference/type-and-representation.html).

